    "result_text": "Done. The full build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the disposable-domain blocklist as a dedicated pipeline step, keeping each class to a single responsibility rather than overloading the existing email-normalization step.\n\n**New files:**\n- `RejectDisposableEmail.java` \u2014 an owner function that reads the (already normalized/lower-cased) email via `@Val`, extracts the domain, and throws when it's on the blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`). Absent email is allowed.\n- `DisposableEmailException.java` \u2014 a checked escalation mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailException`.\n- `DisposableEmailExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `400` with `{\"errors\":[\"email\"]}`, consistent with the invalid-email handler.\n- `...DisposableEmailException.yml` \u2014 escalation wiring.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted `rejectDisposableEmail` right after `normalizeEmail` in both `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml` (carrying `govern: [ transaction ]` on PUT to match its neighbours), so syntax is validated and the address lower-cased before the domain check runs.\n\nDesign notes: I placed the check after normalization so the domain comparison is against a canonical lower-cased address and reuses the syntax guarantee from `NormalizeOwnerEmail` rather than re-parsing/validating. I kept it separate from `NormalizeOwnerEmail` because syntactic normalization and policy-based blocklisting are distinct concerns, and added its own exception/handler pair following the established escalation convention instead of overloading `InvalidEmailException`.",
